I don't have the HyperFrames API in the facts you gave me, so this is a design plus pseudocode. Map it onto whatever seek/timeline primitive HyperFrames exposes.

## Seekability rule

Every visual property is a pure function of the scene time `t`. Use no wall-clock timers, no `setTimeout`, no accumulated state, and no unseeded randomness. Jumping to any `t` then renders the same frame as playing through to it.

## Timeline (1.4 s total)

| Time (s) | Element | Motion |
|---|---|---|
| 0.00 – 0.60 | Heading | Clip-path reveal, left to right, with a 24 px upward slide and opacity 0→1 |
| 0.30 – 0.90 | Heading letters (optional) | 30 ms stagger per word, not per character, to keep it cheap |
| 0.50 – 1.10 | Card 1 | Translate Y 40→0, opacity 0→1, scale 0.96→1 |
| 0.65 – 1.25 | Card 2 | Same, offset by 0.15 s |
| 0.80 – 1.40 | Card 3 | Same, offset by 0.30 s |

Easing is `easeOutCubic` for everything. It has no overshoot, so there is nothing to simulate and no spring state to rebuild when seeking.

## Pseudocode

```js
const clamp01 = x => Math.min(1, Math.max(0, x));
const easeOut = x => 1 - Math.pow(1 - x, 3);
const prog = (t, start, dur) => easeOut(clamp01((t - start) / dur));

function render(t) {
  // Heading
  const h = prog(t, 0, 0.6);
  heading.style.clipPath = `inset(0 ${(1 - h) * 100}% 0 0)`;
  heading.style.opacity = h;
  heading.style.transform = `translateY(${(1 - h) * 24}px)`;

  // Cards
  cards.forEach((el, i) => {
    const p = prog(t, 0.5 + i * 0.15, 0.6);
    el.style.opacity = p;
    el.style.transform = `translateY(${(1 - p) * 40}px) scale(${0.96 + 0.04 * p})`;
  });
}
```

Register `render` as the scene's per-time callback, so play, scrub, and jump all call `render(t)` and nothing else.

## Seek checks

- **Before 0:** `clamp01` pins everything to the hidden start state.
- **After 1.4 s:** everything holds at its final state.
- **Mid-jump:** `render(0.7)` called cold should match the frame reached by playing to 0.7.
- **Backwards seek:** works, because no state carries between frames.

If HyperFrames provides its own timeline object (GSAP-style, paused and driven by `seek(t)`), build the same tracks on it with `ease: "power3.out"` and the same start offsets. Avoid tweens that depend on the previous frame's values.

